1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:08,676
As the long exile waned, a deer bounded off with a Brāhmaṇa's sacred fire-sticks caught in its antlers.

2
00:00:08,676 --> 00:00:11,065
The five Pāṇḍava brothers gave chase —

3
00:00:11,065 --> 00:00:14,774
until it melted into shadow, leaving them lost and parched.

4
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:21,383
Nakula, youngest and swift, found a still and shining lake.

5
00:00:21,383 --> 00:00:27,138
As he stooped to drink, a voice rose from the empty air: "This water is mine.

6
00:00:27,138 --> 00:00:29,380
Answer me first — then drink."

7
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,621
Heedless with thirst, Nakula drank — and fell.

8
00:00:34,621 --> 00:00:38,959
One by one his brothers followed: Sahadeva, Arjuna, Bhīma.

9
00:00:38,959 --> 00:00:45,392
Each ignored the voice, drank, and lay still — four brothers fallen like felled trees.

10
00:00:48,592 --> 00:00:54,704
At last Yudhiṣṭhira came, and found all four fallen. He grieved — yet saw no wound.

11
00:00:54,704 --> 00:01:01,257
When the bodiless voice rose again above the still water, he bowed his head: "Ask, Yakṣa.

12
00:01:01,257 --> 00:01:02,362
I will answer."

13
00:01:04,963 --> 00:01:10,256
"What is swifter than the wind?" — "The mind." "What is heavier than the earth?" —

14
00:01:10,256 --> 00:01:13,742
"A mother." Riddle upon riddle, he answered every one.

15
00:01:13,742 --> 00:01:17,163
Then came the deepest: "What is the greatest wonder?"

16
00:01:19,362 --> 00:01:24,561
And Yudhiṣṭhira answered: "Day upon day, the dead are borne to the House of Death —

17
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:29,510
yet those who remain believe they will never die. That is the greatest wonder."

18
00:01:32,910 --> 00:01:40,435
The crane blazed and put off its shape: it was Dharma, his own divine father, who made the whole trial to prove him.

19
00:01:40,435 --> 00:01:45,495
Pleased, he raised all four brothers, and gave the Pāṇḍavas their year unseen.
